From 4a6375aa5afde12cc3d7db28a769eaaa145383bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tim003 <twong03@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 08:01:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Track observer coverage as spans so a failed observer is
 retried
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Observers run concurrently over consecutive slices, but coverage was tracked
as a single furthest-end marker: the max committed coversUpToId, OR'd with an
in-memory dispatch marker that was never rolled back.

That loses history. If observer A (start, raw-2] dies while observer B
(raw-2, raw-4] succeeds, the watermark jumps to raw-4. A's slice is skipped
for the rest of the process, and because B's marker is committed to the
ledger, it stays skipped after a restart too — session_start only clears the
in-memory marker. The conversation in that span is never observed.

Coverage is now a set of half-open spans (fromId, upToId], and the watermark
is the end of the chain that runs unbroken from the start of the branch. A
later sibling's success can no longer bridge a hole, so the next trigger
re-selects the dead observer's slice.

- observations entries additionally record coversFromId; entries written
  before this change have no start marker and are read as chaining from the
  beginning, so existing ledgers keep their current watermark.
- runtime.dispatchedCoversUpToId becomes runtime.dispatchedSpans, keyed by
  runId. A span is claimed at dispatch, kept when the run succeeds (an empty
  result still means the slice was looked at, so it is not re-observed on a
  loop) and dropped when the run fails, which is what frees it for retry.
- spans naming entries absent from the branch (a rewound /tree) are ignored
  rather than allowed to bridge a gap.

Found after an observer was SIGTERMed mid-exit: it committed nothing, so no
gap opened that time, but a sibling commit would have buried the slice.
